ng  from the big city to be with him  george clare always gets exactly what he  wants there's even a subtle hint that  catherine  may have had an abortion which she was  pressured into by  george she pulls out a weird fetus-like  thing from the drain in one of her  nightmares as george watches behind her  she's more spiritual and religious so it  might not be as big of a deal for  george than her her mother says on the  phone try to remember how he did right  before she is cut off this suggests he  may have had franny out of wedlock and  he married her because it was the right  thing to do  after all we learn they met as  undergrads in college before they had  built their careers the song eddie plays  on the piano is  someday you'll be sorry by louis  armstrong which she says is quote the  story of her life catherine has created  her  own prison yeah we all choose our own  prison  willis also has a difficult history with  men after having sex with george she  asks herself why she keeps doing this  she believes all men are like her father  manipulative with ill intentions  dad made a fortune defending all of them  thieves  con men psychopaths and they all got up  because that's the way the world works  for men there seems to be no justice for  the women in this film their histories  literally erased by powerful  manipulative men  it's ironic then that a woman named  justine is the one to deliver justice to  george you may have also heard mozart's  lacrimosa play shortly before  catherine's death  this is the piece of music she finds  under the piano when she first  arrives at the home mozart died while  composing this piece which was  originally commissioned to commemorate  count von valssag's dead wife on the  anniversary of her death this  foreshadowing is also echoed through the  use of  animals a vulture-like bird is seen on  the top of her chimney and the night  before her murder a raven smashes  through her window  both creatures synonymous with death  although